 ## Background
-The need for distributed training in 3DGS arises from the quadratic growth of Gaussian primitives with scene complexity, particularly in city-scale datasets. Early attempts to parallelize 3DGS relied on spatial partitioning, which proved inefficient due to the non-uniform distribution of camera views and Gaussian density. The introduction of index-parity sharding, notably in BlitzGS, demonstrated that distributing the model globally rather than spatially could eliminate boundary artifacts and communication bottlenecks associated with spatial blocks [S2, unofficial]. Concurrently, inference-focused frameworks like GS-Cache and LS-Gaussian have extended sharding and caching concepts to real-time rendering, addressing inter-frame and inter-tile redundancies respectively [S1].
+The need for distributed training in 3DGS arises from the quadratic growth of Gaussian primitives required for high-fidelity city-scale reconstruction, which exceeds the memory and compute capacity of single GPUs. Early attempts at spatial partitioning revealed significant inefficiencies due to boundary effects and redundant optimization [S2, unofficial]. BlitzGS introduced index-parity sharding to solve these issues, demonstrating that distributing the global model by index parity avoids the geometric constraints of spatial blocks [S2, unofficial]. Concurrently, research into inference acceleration, such as GS-Cache and LS-Gaussian, has focused on reducing redundancy in structured Gaussian representations and hardware-aware workload balancing [S1].
 
